      "title": "The relative error and its use for the spectral block",
      "anchor": "global-word-energy-proof",
      "statement": "The selected rank and level inequalities force the square-root coefficient to have square ≤1+b⁻⁶. For distinct selected words the resulting squared Hilbert–Schmidt norm is at most (1+b⁻⁶)dᵤdᵥ/D*.",
      "hypotheses": "The closed family hypotheses include the counting, local, rank-gap, convergence, size, frame and coupling data. The numerical stage requires b≥16, k≥32, f≤k, 4r+12≤k, both gᵢ≥(kb)⁸, 70 log b≤k, 1≤5cf log k, and 8Cₙ(2r+2)⁵f log b≤k; also the row and mass inputs of the two-word estimate. The passage to the full norm uses the zero nonidentity subseries, the kernel expansion and the norm identification.",
      "definitions": [
        "h=b⁻⁷, P=(1+3/g₁)^f(1+3/g₂)^f and A=(1+δ)e₁.",
        "ε=exp(−ckf log k) and ε′=2^(−(k−2r−5))exp(Cₙ(2r+2)⁵f log b); the selected c is 1/3.",
